==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fr|en}} {{m|fr|bigot||a sanctimonious person; a religious hypocrite}}, from {{etyl|frm|en}} {{m|frm|bigot}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|bigot}}.  Prior history unclear.  

One popular theory states that the word derives from an Old French derogatory term applied to the religiously zealous Normans, who were said to frequently use of the oath "by God" (uttered in an unspecified Germanic language of the time: compare {{etyl|ang|-}} {{m|ang||bī god}}, {{etyl|gmh|-}} {{m|gmh||bī got}}, {{etyl|dum|-}} {{m|dum||bi gode}}), and which can be compared with the similarly used French term {{m|frm|goddamns}} to refer to the English during Joan of Arc's time.  Liberman however dismisses this as having "too strong a taste of a folk etymological guess invented in retrospect" and supports instead Grammont et al.'s derivation of the Old French word from {{m|fro|Albigot||[[Albigensian]] heretic}} [http://blog.oup.com/2011/10/bigot-2/].

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈbɪɡət/|lang=en}}, {{enPR|bĭgʹət}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{label|en|derogatory}} One who is obstinately or [[intolerant]]ly devoted to his or her own opinions and [[prejudice]]s.
#: ''Don't call me a '''bigot'''. That's extremely rude!''
# One who is strongly [[partial]] to one's own [[group]] (e.g. [[religion]], [[race]], [[gender]], [[political]] [[party]], etc.) and is [[intolerant]] of those who differ.

